### Look, or dream? {#look-intuition}

Model predictive control has a built-in defence against compounding errors: every plan starts from the latest *real* observation, so a planning error lasts at most one step. To see how much that matters, run the same planner twice with the same model. Once it looks at the real puck before every replan. Once it never looks: it replans from the state its own model predicted, so the entire run happens in imagination, and the chosen pushes are then replayed on the real table.

In imagination, every one of the 9 dreamed runs ends within 0\.02 of the goal. Replayed on the real table, the same pushes miss by 4\.8 on average with the 250-transition models, 0\.64 with 1,000 and 0\.29 with 4,000. Looking fixes it: with 1,000 or more transitions every run that looks ends within 0\.02 of the goal (@fig-table). With 250 transitions even looking fails in some runs (worst miss 2\.2): feedback cannot rescue a model that is wrong about which way to go.


::: {#cell-fig-table .cell execution_count=5}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
